        Do not fade this whole (very tall) section. view() range is
        entry→cover 28%, so the heading + first card stay near opacity 0
        while only the top is on screen — and a parent fade × card fade
        makes the copy nearly invisible. Intro + each acc-item fade alone.
      */}
      <div class="fx-list-intro scroll-fade-in">
        <h2 class="fx-list-h2">Baseline baked in</h2>
        <p class="fx-list-lead">
          {company.name} comes with 90% of what you need on day zero — including a virtual
          assistant and a learning layer that picks up your playbooks as you go. We customize
          the other 10% to bridge the gaps in a spaghetti network of services — giving you
          a truly unified, all-in-one business operating system.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="acc-list" data-acc-list>
        <div class="acc-item acc-item--ninety scroll-fade-in" data-acc-item id="feature-ninety">
          <button type="button" class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="true">
            <span class="acc-icon acc-icon--ninety"><Puzzle size={20} strokeWidth={1.75} aria-hidden="true" /></span>
            <span class="acc-heading">
              <span class="acc-title">
                A 90% app — everything you need to run the business
                <span class="acc-badge">90%</span>
              </span>
              <span class="acc-teaser">
                Day one: CRM, portal, inbox, scheduling, AI, chat / commands, media library, and
                mobile — live. The last 10% is whatever makes this client uniquely theirs,
                and it bolts on without a rewrite.
              </span>
            </span>
            <span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"><ChevronDown size={18} strokeWidth={2} /></span>
          </button>
          <div class="acc-panel">
            <div class="acc-panel-inner">
              <div class="ninety-meter" role="img" aria-label="90 percent core platform, 10 percent custom additions">
                <div class="ninety-meter-track">
                  <div class="ninety-meter-core">
                    <span class="ninety-meter-label">90%</span>
                    <span class="ninety-meter-name">Core OS</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="ninety-meter-custom">
                    <span class="ninety-meter-label">+10%</span>
                    <span class="ninety-meter-name">Bolt-ons</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <p>
                Most platforms hand you a blank canvas and a monthly bill. {company.name}
                ships the opposite: a complete operating system for running a service
                business — contacts, projects, client portal, billing, smart inbox,
                scheduling, AI agent, chat / commands, media library, Agentic Website Editor, newsletters,
                social & web metrics, read receipts, Siri, white-label branding, and mobile
                push — all wired together on install.
              </p>
              <p>
                That covers what nearly every client actually needs to operate. The
                remaining slice is industry-specific or client-specific: a dealership
                inventory wizard, fleet GPS, a custom intake flow, a one-off integration.
                Those pieces snap onto the same core instead of replacing it.
              </p>
              <ul class="acc-bullets">
                <li>Go live with the full core stack — not a phased rollout of "phase two features"</li>
                <li>Enable industry plugins in one config line when this business needs them</li>
                <li>Custom workflows and integrations build on the same APIs, agent tools, and knowledge base</li>
                <li>No forked codebase — your 10% ships as plugins and config, not a separate product</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        {
          before.map((s) => {
            const Icon = s.icon;
